::: {.callout-note title="Sources"}
**CPH** (Laaksonen, *Competitive Programmer's Handbook*) ch. 9: § 9.1 static array queries (prefix sums, two-dimensional prefix sums, minimum queries with a sparse table), § 9.2–9.3 binary indexed trees and segment trees — what you reach for once the array itself changes · **CSES** problem set: 1646 Static Range Sum Queries, 1652 Forest Queries · **USACO Guide** (Silver): prefix sums and more prefix sums · **CP-Algorithms**: sparse table, Fenwick tree, segment tree (the general answers when an operation has no inverse or the array changes) · LeetCode-style interview patterns: subarray-sum counting with a hash map, product except self, split points. Covered here: range sums and range counts, modular prefix sums, difference arrays (dense, two-dimensional, and sparse event sweeps), prefix sums with a hash map (counts, remainders, first-index maps), prefix and suffix tables for XOR, product, min and max, and split points.
:::

::: {.callout-tip title="What you will be able to do"}
- Build a prefix-sum array in one pass and answer *any* range sum in $O(1)$, writing the range as half-open $[\ell, r)$ with no off-by-one at either end.
- Recognise a **static** range-sum problem from its constraints ($n, q$ both up to $10^5$) and reject the $O(nq)$ nested loop before you write it.
- Turn "add $v$ to every cell of $[\ell, r)$, $q$ times" into two point updates per query with a **difference array**, then rebuild the whole array in $O(n)$.
- Count subarrays whose sum equals $k$, is divisible by $k$, or equals the subarray's own length, using a prefix sum indexed by a **hash map** — and find the *longest* such subarray by storing first indices instead of counts.
- Decide whether a prefix sum helps for an operation *other* than addition — XOR, product, minimum, maximum — by asking the one question that matters: **does it have an inverse?**
- Write $O(n)$ solutions for split-point problems (pivot index, two equal halves, three equal parts) by comparing a prefix against a suffix.
:::


## Why this matters

::: {.callout-note title="In plain words" }
Adding up a range is the most-repeated question in array problems. Answering it by re-adding the range every time costs $O(n)$ per question; answering it from a table of **prefix sums** costs $O(1)$, because you add the whole array once and then only *subtract two numbers*. The trick has a mirror image: the **difference array**, which makes "add the same thing to a whole range" cost $O(1)$ instead of $O(n)$, so a thousand range updates become two thousand point updates and one final pass.
:::

Here is the shape of the argument you will make in an interview. You have $n = 2\cdot10^5$ numbers and $q = 2\cdot10^5$ questions of the form "what is the sum from $a$ to $b$?". Re-adding gives up to $n \cdot q = 4\cdot10^{10}$ additions — on the budget of @sec-cx-budget ($10^8$ to $10^9$ simple operations a second in C++) that is somewhere between a minute and several minutes, against a time limit of one or two seconds. But write $P[i] = a_0 + a_1 + \dots + a_{i-1}$ once, and each question becomes $P[b] - P[a]$: two reads and a subtraction. Same answers, about $n + q = 4\cdot10^5$ operations in total — some $10^5$ times less work — and the entire extra cost is one $O(n)$ pass you do up front. That is the whole technique — **pay once in a table, then charge $O(1)$ per query**.

Prefix sums are also the quiet engine behind much harder things. Counting subarrays with a property becomes counting *equal pairs of prefix sums* with a hash map (@sec-pref-hash). "How many flights use this seat range?" becomes a difference array (@sec-pref-diff). A two-dimensional grid question becomes four lookups and an inclusion–exclusion argument (@sec-pref-2d). And when the operation is not addition — XOR, minimum, product — the same table either works beautifully or fails completely, and knowing *which* is a one-sentence test (@sec-pref-other).

**Where this chapter sits.** @sec-arr built the physical model — contiguous memory, half-open ranges ($[\ell, r)$: start included, end excluded), the cost model, one-pass scanning with running state — and this chapter adds the first real *precomputed table* on top of it. The running total of @sec-arr-scan is kept instead of thrown away, and that one change answers every range question in constant time. The next chapter, @sec-hash, takes the hash map that appears here in @sec-pref-hash and makes it the main character; later chapters on two pointers, sliding windows and binary search keep using the prefix table as a building block.

The chapter has two parts. Part A is six lessons, each with its convention written out precisely and its formula checked by code. Part B is six patterns, each with two or three fully solved, stress-tested problems. If a formula feels weightless, run the cell under it and print the array it talks about.

