## Part A — Lessons

## A1. Prefix sums: the table and the off-by-one-free convention {#sec-pref-def}

::: {.callout-note title="In plain words"}
A prefix sum is the running total you would write in the margin while adding a column of numbers: after each row you note the total so far. Then the sum of rows 3 through 7 is "the total after row 7" minus "the total after row 2" — you never re-add anything.
:::

There are two ways to index the table, and this book uses the one that removes every off-by-one error. Define

$$P[0] = 0, \qquad P[i+1] = P[i] + a[i] \quad (0 \le i < n),$$ {#eq-pref-build}

so $P$ has length $n+1$, and cell $P[i]$ is the sum of everything *strictly before* index $i$. Then the sum of the half-open range $[\ell, r)$ — the exact range @sec-arr-ranges taught us to write — is

$$\sum_{k=\ell}^{r-1} a[k] \;=\; P[r] - P[\ell].$$ {#eq-pref-range}

Both ends obey the same rule: the *exclusive* end of a range is the cell you subtract. Nothing is counted twice and nothing is skipped, because the range $[\ell,r)$ contains exactly the cells that are inside $[0,r)$ and not inside $[0,\ell)$.

::: {#c1f5d2a6 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def build_prefix(a):
    p = [0] * (len(a) + 1)
    for i, x in enumerate(a):
        p[i + 1] = p[i] + x          # eq-pref-build, literally
    return p


def range_sum(p, l, r):
    return p[r] - p[l]               # sum of a[l:r]

a = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2]
p = build_prefix(a)
print("a =", a)
print("P =", p)
for l, r in [(0, 7), (2, 5), (1, 2), (3, 3), (0, 0), (4, 7)]:
    got, want = range_sum(p, l, r), sum(a[l:r])
    print(f"P[{r}] - P[{l}] = {p[r]} - {p[l]} = {got:>3}   direct: {want}")
    assert got == want
print("all ranges agree, including the empty ones")
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}
```
a = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2]
P = [0, 3, 4, 8, 9, 14, 23, 25]
P[7] - P[0] = 25 - 0 =  25   direct: 25
P[5] - P[2] = 14 - 4 =  10   direct: 10
P[2] - P[1] = 4 - 3 =   1   direct: 1
P[3] - P[3] = 8 - 8 =   0   direct: 0
P[0] - P[0] = 0 - 0 =   0   direct: 0
P[7] - P[4] = 25 - 9 =  16   direct: 16
all ranges agree, including the empty ones
```
:::
:::


Note the two empty cases: $(3,3)$ and $(0,0)$ both give $0$ with no special-casing, because $P[x]-P[x]=0$. A single-element range $[i, i{+}1)$ costs one subtraction too. Compare the alternative convention, $P[i] = a[0]+\dots+a[i]$ (inclusive, length $n$): there the same query is $P[r-1]-P[\ell-1]$, which needs a guard for $\ell = 0$ and an uncomfortable $-1$ on both ends. It is a small thing until you have ten range queries in one problem, and then it is the wrong-answer verdict. **The invariant worth memorising** is the sentence that makes @eq-pref-build and @eq-pref-range the same fact:

> **Invariant.** For every $i$, $P[i] = \sum a[0:i)$.

::: {#fig-pref-build}

