Building the table one cell at a time — a film strip of @eq-pref-build. At step $i$ the new cell $P[i{+}1]$ is the old total plus $a[i]$, so the filled (indigo) part is exactly the range $[0, i{+}1)$ covered so far. This is @sec-arr-scan's "one pass with running state", with the twist that we *keep* every intermediate value instead of throwing it away.

:::

What the table buys, in one line of arithmetic: building it costs $O(n)$ time and $O(n)$ space, and each later query costs $O(1)$. So $q$ questions cost $O(n + q)$ instead of $O(nq)$. The trade is *space for time*, and it is one of the cheapest trades in programming — which is why prefix sums are the first thing to try whenever a problem asks about many ranges of an unchanging array.

::: {#fig-pref-range}

