**Difference arrays also go two-dimensional.** One rectangle update $[r_1,r_2) \times [c_1,c_2)$ becomes four point updates — $+v$ at $(r_1,c_1)$, $-v$ at $(r_2,c_1)$, $-v$ at $(r_1,c_2)$, $+v$ at $(r_2,c_2)$ — and a *double* prefix pass rebuilds the grid. The signs are the mirror image of @eq-pref-2d-query: the corner you add to is the one the rectangle starts at, and the far corner comes back positive because it was cancelled twice. A later chapter on multidimensional and higher-order prefix sums develops that case in full, together with higher dimensions; here it is enough to see that the two techniques are one technique seen from opposite sides, and to check it once in code.

::: {#dade75da .cell execution_count=13}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def rect_add_2d(m, n, updates):
    """Rectangle adds [r1,r2) x [c1,c2) += v, by four writes each and a 2D prefix rebuild."""
    d = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(m + 1)]
    for r1, c1, r2, c2, v in updates:
        d[r1][c1] += v; d[r1][c2] -= v
        d[r2][c1] -= v; d[r2][c2] += v
    out = [[0] * n for _ in range(m)]
    for i in range(m):
        for j in range(n):            # 2D prefix of d, the same recurrence as eq-pref-2d-build
            up = out[i - 1][j] if i else 0
            left = out[i][j - 1] if j else 0
            diag = out[i - 1][j - 1] if i and j else 0
            out[i][j] = d[i][j] + up + left - diag
    return out


for _ in range(300):
    m, n = random.randint(1, 5), random.randint(1, 5)
    ups = []
    for _ in range(random.randint(0, 4)):
        r1 = random.randint(0, m); c1 = random.randint(0, n)
        ups.append((r1, c1, random.randint(r1, m), random.randint(c1, n), random.randint(-3, 3)))
    want = [[sum(v for r1, c1, r2, c2, v in ups if r1 <= i < r2 and c1 <= j < c2)
             for j in range(n)] for i in range(m)]
    assert rect_add_2d(m, n, ups) == want
print("2D difference array: four writes per rectangle, one 2D prefix pass")
```

