## A5. Prefix and suffix of other operations: XOR, product, min, max {#sec-pref-other}

::: {.callout-note title="In plain words"}
Everything in @sec-pref-def secretly relied on being able to *undo* the accumulation: $P[r]-P[\ell]$ works because subtraction reverses addition. So before you build a prefix table for some other operation, ask one question — **is there an inverse?** XOR: yes, it is its own inverse. Product: division, but zeros and modular arithmetic complicate it. Minimum, maximum, GCD: no inverse at all, so a prefix table cannot answer a middle range — only ranges that start at an end.
:::

Say it precisely. A prefix table answers an arbitrary range query in $O(1)$ when the operation forms a **group**: it is associative, has an identity, and every element has an inverse. Addition qualifies (identity $0$, inverse $-x$), so @eq-pref-range applies. So does XOR (identity $0$, every element is its own inverse since $x \oplus x = 0$):

$$\bigoplus_{k=\ell}^{r-1} a[k] \;=\; P[\ell] \oplus P[r], \qquad P[i+1] = P[i] \oplus a[i].$$ {#eq-pref-xor}

Notice the range formula uses $\oplus$ on *both* ends — no subtraction exists to get wrong, and the same code shape carries over.

Multiplication needs division as its inverse. With non-zero integers the range product $P[r] / P[\ell]$ is an exact division, but two things go wrong in practice. A single $0$ makes every later prefix product $0$ and destroys the information needed to recover a range; and the products themselves overflow almost immediately. The interview-standard fix (@sec-pref-p5-a) sidesteps division entirely by combining a **prefix** product with a **suffix** product. Under a modulus, dividing means multiplying by a modular inverse, which exists only when the divisor is coprime to the modulus — a number-theory topic for later.

Minimum, maximum and GCD are the interesting failures. They are associative and have an identity, but nothing undoes them: from $\max(a[0{:}r])$ you cannot recover $\max(a[\ell{:}r])$, because the value you would need to "un-take" may not even be in the range. Two responses, and they are worth telling apart in an interview:

- **Restrict the ranges.** Prefix minima/maxima and **suffix** minima/maxima are still perfectly good, because a range anchored at an end needs no undoing. Walking from the right, the running maximum is exactly what identifies *leaders* — elements that are at least everything to their right.
- **Change data structure.** For arbitrary ranges with no inverse you need a **sparse table** (the $\max$-over-sliding-powers-of-two idempotent trick), a **segment tree**, or a **monotonic deque** for sliding windows. All appear later in this book; the point here is knowing *why* you are being pushed off prefix sums.

::: {#fig-pref-ops}

