## Pattern 4: Two-dimensional prefix sums {#sec-pref-p4}

**From a line to a grid.** Everything so far lived on one axis. On a grid the same table holds the sums of rectangles anchored at the corner, and inclusion–exclusion turns any rectangle into four reads.

**Recognise it.** A grid, static or read-only, and questions about rectangles: "sum of this sub-rectangle", "is this square's average at least $x$", "largest square under a budget". If a brute-force answer loops over the rows *and* columns of the query, build the table once and answer in four reads.

::: {.callout-important title="Template"}
One extra row and one extra column of zeros, then @eq-pref-2d-build and @eq-pref-2d-query.

```text
for i in range(m):
    rowsum = 0
    for j in range(n):
        rowsum += a[i][j]
        P[i+1][j+1] = P[i][j+1] + rowsum

def rect(r1, c1, r2, c2):           # half-open [r1,r2) x [c1,c2)
    return P[r2][c2] - P[r1][c2] - P[r2][c1] + P[r1][c1]
```

**Invariant.** $P[i][j]$ is the sum of the $i\times j$ block anchored at the top-left corner, so the four reads of a query subtract two strips and give back the corner they took twice.
:::

**Complexity.** Build $O(mn)$; query $O(1)$ instead of $O(mn)$ — the whole rectangle becomes four array reads and three signs. Space $O(mn)$, which for a $300\times300$ grid is trivial and for a $10^4\times10^4$ one is not: then stream the rows and keep only the running column sums.

