## Pattern 6: Split points {#sec-pref-p6}

**From given ranges to choosing the cut.** In every pattern so far the ranges were given or counted. Now we choose where to cut the array so that the parts' sums satisfy a relation, and the prefix sum makes each candidate cut $O(1)$.

**Recognise it.** The task is a **cut**: divide the array into two or three contiguous parts whose aggregates satisfy a relation — equal, one at least the other, balanced. A pivot, a fair split, "split the array so that the left sum is at least the right". Once the prefix table exists, every cut position is two reads, so all $n-1$ cuts cost $O(n)$.

::: {.callout-important title="Template"}
Decide whether the element *at* the cut belongs to the left part, the right part, or neither, and write that down once.

```text
total = P[n]
# two parts, left = a[0:k], right = a[k:n],  k in 1..n-1
for k in range(1, n):
    left, right = P[k], total - P[k]
    test(left, right)

# three parts, left = a[0:i], middle = a[i:j], right = a[j:n]
for i in range(n + 1):
    for j in range(i, n + 1):
        test(P[i], P[j] - P[i], total - P[j])      # O(n) when the predicate is monotone
```

**Invariant.** $P[k]$ is the whole left part and `total - P[k]` the whole right part, so a cut test never re-sums anything; and because the two sum to `total`, "left $=$ right" is the same test as "$P[k] = \text{total}/2$".
:::

**Complexity.** $O(n)$ for two-part tests, $O(n)$ or $O(n\log n)$ for three-part tests, versus $O(n^2)$ or $O(n^3)$ for re-summing each side. The space is one table, or zero if you keep a running `left` and the total.

